= A
{parent=2007}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Mathematics and the environment}

= 1
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

In the non-scattering <grey atmosphere> approximation, $I$ is the frequency-integrated <specific intensity> along a ray, $s$ is distance along that ray, $\rho$ is the <mass density>, and $\kappa$ is the <mass absorption coefficient>. The <local thermodynamic equilibrium> source is the frequency-integrated <Planck function> $B=\sigma_{\rm SB}T^4/\pi$. Across a thin element $ds$, absorption removes $\kappa\rho I\,ds$ and thermal emission adds $\kappa\rho B\,ds$. Their balance gives $dI/ds=-\kappa\rho(I-B)$.

Take the upward vertical as the polar axis, put $\mu=\cos\theta$, and define downward <optical depth> by
$$
\boxed{\tau(z)=\int_z^\infty\kappa(z')\rho(z')\,dz'.}
$$
Along a ray, $dz/ds=\mu$ and $d\tau/ds=-\kappa\rho\mu$. The <chain rule> therefore gives $\boxed{\mu I_\tau=I-B}$. In a plane-parallel, azimuthally symmetric field, the <solid angle> element is $d\omega=d\varphi\,d\mu$, so
$$
\boxed{J(\tau)=\frac12\int_{-1}^{1}I(\tau,\mu)\,d\mu.}
$$
<Local radiative equilibrium> requires zero net radiative heating of the material: absorption $4\pi\kappa\rho J$ balances emission $4\pi\kappa\rho B$. Where the absorption coefficient is nonzero this gives $B=J$, and substitution gives the required angular <radiative transfer equation>. It does not require the <specific intensity> itself to be isotropic.

At the top of an atmosphere with no external incoming long-wave radiation, impose $\boxed{I(0,\mu)=0\quad(-1\leq\mu<0)}$. These are inward directions; prescribing the same zero value for outward directions would remove the emergent radiation as well. The upward <radiative flux> and its derivative are
$$
\Phi=2\pi\int_{-1}^{1}\mu I\,d\mu,\qquad
\frac{d\Phi}{d\tau}=2\pi\int_{-1}^{1}(I-B)\,d\mu=4\pi(J-B)=0.
$$
Thus $\boxed{\Phi\text{ is constant with height}}$: <local radiative equilibrium> expresses energy conservation through successive atmospheric layers.

= 2
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The first <Saint-Venant equation> conserves water volume. The second balances material acceleration against downslope gravity, bed friction and the free-surface pressure gradient. With length scale $L$, depth scale $H$, slope $S$ and velocity scale $U$, the conventional gravity-friction scaling has $\varepsilon=H/(SL)$ and <Froude number> $F=U/\sqrt{gH}$. The pressure-gradient/downslope-gravity ratio is $\varepsilon$, and the inertia/downslope-gravity ratio is $\varepsilon F^2$.

The uniform steady solution obeys $u^2=h$ and $hu=Q$, hence $\boxed{u_0=Q^{1/3},\ h_0=Q^{2/3}}$. In the <constant-flux river momentum relaxation> reduction, substitute $h=Q/u$ and $X=1-s$. Since $u_s=-u_X$ and $h_s=Qu_X/u^2$, the resulting <quasilinear partial differential equation> is
$$
\boxed{u_t+c(u)u_X=\frac{1-u^3/Q}{\varepsilon F^2},\qquad c(u)=\frac{Q}{F^2u^2}-u.}
$$
A downstream boundary at $X=0$ supplies data into $X>0$ precisely when $c(u_1)>0$, equivalent to $\boxed{u_1<Q^{1/3}/F^{2/3}}$. Although water flows out there, the relevant <characteristic curves> carry information upstream in this subcritical regime.

Along a <characteristic curve>, $dX/dt=c(u)$ and $du/dt=(1-u^3/Q)/(\varepsilon F^2)$. Positive $u$ relaxes toward $u_0$; near $u_0$, its displacement decays at rate $3/(\varepsilon F^2u_0)$. Boundary-generated deviations therefore become small after a time of order $\varepsilon$ and, while the characteristic speed stays bounded away from zero, within a distance of order $\varepsilon$. The outer state is the initial uniform solution. A jump between initial and boundary values at their corner can produce a weak solution or a thin initial adjustment; a globally smooth corner solution requires compatible data.

For the moving boundary put $Y=X-vt$. The characteristic speed relative to it is $c(u)-v$, while the outer speed is
$$
\boxed{v_b=c(u_0)=Q^{1/3}(F^{-2}-1).}
$$
For the intended tidal slowing $0<u_1\leq u_0$, $c$ is decreasing in $u$ and hence $c(u)\geq v_b$ throughout the relaxation. Thus $v<v_b$ leaves an incoming family and an attached thin <boundary layer>. The diagram shows numerical characteristics of this scalar reduction, with a slower boundary velocity. When $v>v_b$, the tidal boundary overtakes the unperturbed characteristic family; an attached smooth relaxation layer is no longer appropriate, and a <tidal bore> or <shock wave> can arise in a fuller river model.

\Image[2007-moving-tide-characteristics.png]
{title=Characteristics leaving a slowly advancing tidal boundary}
{description=Original calculation for the scalar momentum reduction, with $Q=1$, $F=0.7$, $\varepsilon=0.04$, $u_1=0.65$ and $v=0.4<v_b$. Curves approach the background characteristic speed after a short relaxation; this is not an exact solution of the full mass-and-momentum system.}

There are two qualifications to the printed reduction. First, without the physical slowing assumption, $v<v_b$ alone need not make the boundary incoming: for $u_1>u_0$, also require $v<c(u_1)$. Second, imposing $hu=Q$ exactly everywhere in the original continuity equation gives $h_t=0$, and consequently $u_t=0$. The time-dependent characteristics above solve the requested momentum-only approximation, rather than the complete <Saint-Venant equation> system with an exactly constant flux.

= 3
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

<Bedload transport> carries sediment by rolling, sliding and short hops near the bed. <Suspended sediment> is held in the water column by turbulent mixing. The competition between particle <settling velocity> and the turbulent vertical velocity scale chiefly determines which description applies; particle size and density influence that settling velocity.

Scale the depths and bed/free-surface elevations by $d$, both velocities by $U$, distance by $L$, and time by $T$. A choice producing exactly the requested coefficients is
$$
\boxed{U=\sqrt{\frac{gSd}{f}},\quad L=\frac{\rho_sa}{f\rho_w},\quad T=\frac{dL}{aU}.}
$$
The sediment continuity equation then has unit coefficients, while both material-acceleration time derivatives acquire the factor $L/(UT)=a/d$. Dividing water momentum by $gd/L$ and sediment momentum by $U^2/L$ gives
$$
\boxed{\varepsilon=\frac ad,\quad F^2=\frac{U^2}{gd}=\frac Sf,\quad
\delta=\frac{SL}{d}=\frac{\rho_s}{\rho_w}\varepsilon F^2,\quad
\kappa=\frac{f_bL}{U}.}
$$
For example $d=1\,\mathrm m$, $a=10^{-3}\,\mathrm m$, $S=10^{-3}$, $f=10^{-2}$ and $\rho_s/\rho_w=2.65$ give $\varepsilon=10^{-3}$ and $\delta=2.65\times10^{-4}$. These are illustrative values: small effective mobile-layer thickness and small slope explain the two small parameters.

Set $\varepsilon=\delta=0$ at leading order. Water continuity gives $hu=q$, with spatially constant flux; take a prescribed constant $q$. Water momentum integrates to $\eta+F^2u^2/2=C$. Since $s=\eta-h$,
$$
\boxed{s(u)=C-\frac{F^2u^2}{2}-\frac qu.}
$$
The <bedload transport model with sediment inertia> has remaining equations $s(u)_t+v_x=0$ and $vv_x=(u-v)^2-\kappa v$.

Choose a uniform state of this reduced model with $h_0=u_0=q=1$, constant $s_0=C-F^2/2-1$, and
$$
\boxed{v_0=\frac{2+\kappa-\sqrt{\kappa^2+4\kappa}}2\in(0,1).}
$$
It satisfies $(1-v_0)^2=\kappa v_0$. For small perturbations $U,V$ of the two velocities, put $a_1=2(1-v_0)>0$ and $b_1=a_1+\kappa>0$. <Linearization> gives
$$
(1-F^2)U_t+V_x=0,\qquad v_0V_x=a_1U-b_1V.
$$
For a <Fourier mode> $e^{ikx+\lambda t}$ this yields
$$
\lambda=-\frac{ika_1}{(1-F^2)(b_1+ikv_0)},\qquad
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}\lambda=-\frac{a_1v_0k^2}{(1-F^2)(b_1^2+k^2v_0^2)}.}
$$
Every nonzero mode decays for $F<1$ and grows for $F>1$. The spatially uniform mode is neutral, and $F=1$ is a singular threshold where the neglected terms matter. In a general uniform state the controlling sign is $q/u_0^2-F^2u_0$: equivalently the actual mean-flow <Froude number> is less or greater than one. The displayed $F=1$ criterion uses the stated unit-depth, unit-velocity normalization of the reduced steady state.

= 4
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

<Basal sliding> allows ice to pass over bed obstacles by enhanced deformation and <regelation>: pressure-induced melting on the upstream side and refreezing downstream transfer ice around small obstacles. <Cavitation> opens water-filled gaps on the downstream side. Their size and the remaining ice-bed contact depend on <effective pressure in subglacial drainage> $N=p_{\rm ice}-p_{\rm water}$ as well as basal <shear stress> $\tau$. At fixed $\tau$, reducing $N$ generally enlarges cavities and reduces resistance, giving a sliding law $u(\tau,N)$ with $u_N<0$ in this regime.

A <linked cavity drainage system> connects these cavities through passages along the bed. Under the prescribed decreasing relation $N(Q)$, the <chain rule> gives $\boxed{u_Q=u_NN'(Q)>0}$. This conclusion uses that hydraulic relation; it need not apply to a different drainage regime.

With $t=\varepsilon\tau$, the ice continuity equation becomes $H_\tau+\varepsilon(uH)_x=\varepsilon s'(x)$. For bounded spatial gradients over $\tau=O(1)$, $H$ is independent of the fast seasonal time to leading order. Meanwhile $S'(Q)=\varepsilon\kappa$ gives $S_t=\kappa Q_\tau$, so
$$
\boxed{\kappa Q_\tau+Q_x=M(\tau).}
$$
Let $J(\tau)=\int_0^\tau M(r)\,dr$. Direct differentiation proves that
$$
\boxed{Q(x,\tau)=\frac{J(\tau)-J(\tau-\kappa x)}\kappa}
$$
satisfies the equation and $Q(0,\tau)=0$. This is the boundary-generated solution, or a periodic-forcing solution with the forcing continued into its past. An independently prescribed initial profile would also need matching in the region whose <characteristic curves> have not yet reached the upstream boundary.

If $M>0$, $J$ is invertible and rearranging gives $x=[\tau-J^{-1}(J(\tau)-\kappa Q)]/\kappa$. For $M=1-\delta\cos\tau$ with $|\delta|<1$,
$$
\boxed{J(\tau)=\tau-\delta\sin\tau,\qquad J^{-1}(\theta)=\theta+\delta\sin\theta+O(\delta^2).}
$$
Substitution, followed by the sine-difference identity, gives
$$
\boxed{x=Q+\frac{2\delta}{\kappa}\sin\frac{\kappa Q}{2}\cos\left(\tau-\frac{\kappa Q}{2}\right)+O(\delta^2).}
$$
For fixed $Q$ and nonzero first-order amplitude, the extrema occur at $\tau=\kappa Q/2+n\pi+O(\delta)$. Since $x=Q+O(\delta)$, the <seasonal phase lag in linked cavity drainage> gives the stated phase relation $\boxed{\tau\approx\kappa x/2\pmod\pi}$. Maxima and minima alternate; when $\sin(\kappa Q/2)=0$, the first-order approximation is flat and does not locate individual extrema.

= B
{parent=2007}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Mathematical physiology}

= 5
{parent=b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an experiment beginning by mixing unbound <enzyme> with <substrate>, take $\boxed{[E](0)=e_0>0,\ [S](0)=s_0>0,\ [X](0)=[Y](0)=[E_i](0)=[P](0)=0}$. The <enzyme-substrate complexes>, inactivated enzyme and product are initially absent. Nonzero pre-existing product could be allowed without changing the dynamics here, since it has no feedback reaction in the specified scheme.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write concentrations as $E,S,X,Y,E_i,P$ in this calculation. The <law of mass action> assigns the forward bimolecular rate $k_1ES$ and the first-order rates $k_{-1}X,k_2X,k_3Y,k_4Y$. Each species gains the rates of reactions producing it and loses those consuming it:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\dot E&=-k_1ES+k_{-1}X+k_3Y,&\dot S&=-k_1ES+k_{-1}X,\\
\dot X&=k_1ES-(k_{-1}+k_2)X,&\dot Y&=k_2X-(k_3+k_4)Y,\\
\dot E_i&=k_4Y,&\dot P&=k_3Y.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Adding the equations for all enzyme-bearing species cancels every reaction rate, so $\boxed{E+X+Y+E_i=e_0}$. This is conservation of the total enzyme-derived material, including the inactivated form. It does not assert conservation of active enzyme: <mechanism-based inhibition> transfers some of that conserved material irreversibly into $E_i$.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The conserved total is $e_0=[E](0)$ under the chosen initial conditions. Substitute $E=e_0-X-Y-E_i$ into the equations from the previous part. This closes the subsystem for $S,X,Y,E_i$; product can be recovered afterward by integrating $\dot P=k_3Y$.

Let $k_\Sigma=k_{-1}+k_2$ and $K_m=k_\Sigma/k_1$. The required <nondimensionalization> is completed by
$$
\boxed{\sigma=\frac{s_0}{K_m},\quad\rho=\frac{k_{-1}}{k_2},\quad
\varepsilon=\frac{e_0}{s_0+K_m},\quad
\psi=\frac{k_3+k_4}{k_\Sigma},\quad\phi=\frac{k_4}{k_\Sigma},\quad
T=\varepsilon k_\Sigma t.}
$$
Indeed $X=e_0\sigma x/(1+\sigma)$ and the free-enzyme fraction is $1-\sigma x/(1+\sigma)-y-e_i$. Substitution and division by the indicated time scale give
$$
\begin{aligned}
s_T&=-s[(1+\sigma)-\sigma x-(1+\sigma)y-(1+\sigma)e_i]+\frac\rho{1+\rho}x,\\
\varepsilon x_T&=s[(1+\sigma)-\sigma x-(1+\sigma)y-(1+\sigma)e_i]-x,\\
\varepsilon y_T&=\frac{\sigma}{(1+\sigma)(1+\rho)}x-\psi y,\\
\varepsilon(e_i)_T&=\phi y.
\end{aligned}
$$
For example $k_2/k_\Sigma=1/(1+\rho)$ explains the coefficient in the third equation. The small multiplier on the intermediate-species derivatives identifies their potentially fast adjustment relative to substrate depletion.

= iv
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For <quasi-steady enzyme kinetics at low substrate saturation>, the <quasi-steady-state approximation> is controlled here by $\varepsilon=e_0/(s_0+K_m)$, rather than by $e_0/s_0$ alone. Even with $e_0\approx s_0$, taking $K_m\gg s_0$ makes $\varepsilon\ll1$ and $\sigma=s_0/K_m=O(\varepsilon)$.

After the initial fast layer $T=O(\varepsilon)$, the third equation gives
$$
y\sim\frac{\sigma x}{(1+\sigma)(1+\rho)\psi}=O(\varepsilon).
$$
Since $\phi=O(\varepsilon)$, the last equation gives $(e_i)_T=(\phi/\varepsilon)y=O(\varepsilon)$. Starting from $e_i(0)=0$, this implies $e_i=O(\varepsilon)$ on bounded intervals of the slow time. The quasi-steady second equation then gives $x=s+O(\varepsilon)$. In the first equation,
$$
s_T=-s+\frac\rho{1+\rho}s+O(\varepsilon)=-\frac{s}{1+\rho}+O(\varepsilon).
$$
Matching to the initial substrate value $s(0)=1$ therefore yields $\boxed{s(T)\sim e^{-T/(1+\rho)}}$. The relation $x\sim s$ is an outer approximation: it does not replace the initial layer needed to reconcile $x(0)=0$ with $s(0)=1$. Likewise the smallness of accumulated inactivated enzyme has been justified for bounded slow times, not arbitrarily long times.

= 6
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

For the <travelling wave> coordinate $\xi=x+st$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{s(U'+\gamma V')=\mu-U+\varepsilon U'',\qquad\varepsilon sV'=f(U,V).}
$$
<Fast-slow matching for a calcium pulse> joins two fast transitions, $B\to C$ and $D\to A$, joined by slow portions $C\to D$ and $A\to B$. Write the <nullcline> as $V=g(U)$. The resting point has $U_B=\mu$, $V_B=g(\mu)$ in the leading matched description.

Put $\xi=\varepsilon X$. Multiplying the first equation by $\varepsilon$ gives $U_{XX}=s(U_X+\gamma V_X)+\varepsilon(U-\mu)$. At leading order, integrate and use $U_X\to0$, $(U,V)\to(U_B,V_B)$ at the start of the front. Together with the second equation this gives
$$
\boxed{U_X=s[U-U_B+\gamma(V-V_B)],\qquad sV_X=f(U,V).}
$$
The fast equilibria are intersections of $V=g(U)$ and $V=V_B-(U-U_B)/\gamma$. As the slope $-1/\gamma$ changes, there may be one or three simple intersections, with two distinct intersections at a tangency. At a simple point of the nullcline, $f_U=-f_Vg'$, and the sign convention above/below it gives $f_V<0$. The <Jacobian matrix> of the fast system has
$$
\det J=f_V-\gamma f_U=f_V(1+\gamma g'),\qquad\operatorname{tr}J=s+f_V/s.
$$
For the usual three-intersection configuration, the two outer points have $1+\gamma g'>0$, hence negative determinant and one positive and one negative <eigenvalue>: they are <saddle points>. The middle point has positive determinant; it is linearly stable if $s^2<-f_V$ and unstable if $s^2>-f_V$. Zero trace is a possible oscillatory stability threshold, with a nonlinear classification requiring further information about $f$.

To select the front speed, follow the appropriate branch of the <unstable manifold> of $B$ and vary $s$ until it meets the <stable manifold> of $C$. This is a <shooting method> for a <heteroclinic orbit>. Follow the high-$U$ slow branch from $C$ to $D$; the falling front obeys the same fast equations with the integration constant anchored at $D$. Choose $D$ so that its descending saddle connection reaches a low-$U$ point $A$ at that same speed. The remaining slow branch returns toward the resting point, completing the matched spike. The construction requires the indicated connections; a nullcline sketch alone does not specify their numerical speed or prove their existence for every possible kinetic function $f$.

\Image[2007-calcium-pulse-phase-plane.png]
{title=Fast and slow portions of the calcium-wave phase-plane construction}
{description=A qualitative original sketch: the rising nullcline branches carry the slow return and recovery, while dashed paths indicate the saddle connections that a shooting calculation must find. The paths illustrate the matching construction and are not a numerical solution for a specified kinetic function.}

On the slow portions, $f(U,V)=0$ at leading order, so $V=g(U)$ and $V'=g'(U)U'$. Neglecting the small diffusion term yields
$$
\boxed{s(1+\gamma g'(U))U'=\mu-U.}
$$
On the outer rising branches the coefficient on the left is positive. Recovery from $C$ toward $D$ requires decreasing $U$, hence $\mu<U_D$; the low branch from $A$ back toward rest requires increasing $U$, hence $U_A<\mu$. Therefore $\boxed{U_A<\mu<U_D}$. These inequalities distinguish the recovery branches from a trajectory that would move away from the required matching points.

= 7
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

<Systole> is ventricular contraction and ejection; <diastole> includes ventricular relaxation and filling. Let $V_a,V_v,V_{LV}$ denote arterial, venous and left-ventricular blood volumes. Let $Q^+$ flow through the aortic valve into the arteries, $Q_c$ through the peripheral circulation, and $Q^-$ through the mitral valve into the ventricle. Conservation of volume gives
$$
\boxed{\dot V_a=Q^+-Q_c,\quad\dot V_v=Q_c-Q^-,\quad\dot V_{LV}=Q^--Q^+.}
$$
The same $Q_c$ leaves arteries and enters veins because the capillary compartment has no changing stored volume in this approximation. Adding the equations conserves total modeled blood volume.

Use constant <vascular compliances> $C_a,C_v$, with $\dot V_a=C_a\dot p_a$ and $\dot V_v=C_v\dot p_v$, and <ventricular elastance> $p_{LV}=E_{LV}(t)(V_{LV}-V_0)$. The <hydraulic resistances> and ideal one-way valves give
$$
Q_c=\frac{p_a-p_v}{R_c},\qquad Q^+=\frac{[p_{LV}-p_a]^+}{R_a},\qquad Q^-=\frac{[p_v-p_{LV}]^+}{R_v},
$$
where $[z]^+=\max(z,0)$. Substitution into the conservation equations gives the two stated arterial/venous pressure equations. Since $V_{LV}-V_0=p_{LV}/E_{LV}$, differentiating this identity gives the third equation. The positive parts encode valve closure against a reverse pressure difference.

During isovolumetric contraction both valves are closed. Let $V^+$ be the end-diastolic ventricular volume, $V_0$ its unstressed reference volume, and $p_a^+$ arterial pressure before ejection. The rapidly raised elastance generates $E_s(V^+-V_0)$, so the aortic valve opens if $\boxed{E_s(V^+-V_0)>p_a^+}$.

During ejection, $R_a/R_c\ll1$ makes the open-valve pressure drop small on the peripheral drainage time scale, so $p_{LV}\approx p_a$. The mitral valve is closed, and adding arterial and ventricular volume balances eliminates $Q^+$. With $p_v\ll p_a$ this gives
$$
R_cC_a\dot p_a+R_c\frac{d(p_a/E_{LV})}{dt}\approx-p_a.
$$
For approximately constant systolic elastance, set $C_s=1/E_s$ and reset time to the start of the slow ejection phase. Then
$$
\boxed{p_a(t)\approx\hat p_a\exp\!\left[-\frac{t}{R_c(C_a+C_s)}\right].}
$$
Here $\hat p_a$ is the matched pressure at that start. If peripheral outflow is negligible during the preceding <rapid artery-ventricle pressure equilibration>, conservation gives the more explicit $\hat p_a=[C_ap_a^++V^+-V_0]/(C_a+C_s)$.

After aortic closure the ventricular volume is fixed at the end-systolic value $V^-$. Relaxation decreases $E_{LV}$ until the mitral valve opens at $p_{LV}=p_v$, hence $\boxed{E_{LV}\sim p_v/(V^--V_0)}$, with $p_v$ the venous filling pressure at opening. During filling the aortic valve is closed, so $R_cC_a\dot p_a=-(p_a-p_v)\approx-p_a$. With $p_a^-$ the arterial pressure at the start of filling and time reset there,
$$
\boxed{p_a(t)\approx p_a^-e^{-t/(R_cC_a)}.}
$$
The change from $C_a+C_s$ to $C_a$ is physical: the ejecting ventricle shares the arterial pressure during systole, whereas a closed aortic valve disconnects it during filling.

= 8
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

In this <delayed carbon-dioxide ventilation feedback> model, $p$ is a representative carbon-dioxide partial pressure, $K$ an effective storage capacitance, $M>0$ metabolic production, and $pV$ removal by <pulmonary ventilation>. The delay represents transport and sensing between the gas-exchange compartment and the ventilatory response. The <Hill equation> response rises sigmoidally with pressure, reaches half its maximum $\alpha$ at $p=\theta$, and saturates at $\alpha$; $n>1$ permits a steep threshold-like response.

For positive parameters, $pV(p)$ increases strictly from zero to infinity because its derivative is $V+pV'>0$. Its graph therefore intersects the horizontal line $M$ exactly once: $\boxed{M=p^*V(p^*),\quad p^*>0}$. The figure shows this argument and the first imaginary-root crossing.

\Image[2007-respiratory-delay-threshold.png]
{title=A unique respiratory equilibrium can become unstable through delayed feedback}
{description=Illustrative dimensionless Hill response with $\alpha=1$, $\theta=1$, $n=3$, $K=1$ and equilibrium $p^*=0.8$. The second panel separates the instantaneous-damping and delayed-gain vectors at the first imaginary root.}

For a small perturbation $z=p-p^*$, <linearization> gives
$$
\dot z=-Az(t)-Bz(t-\tau),\qquad A=V(p^*)/K,\quad B=p^*V'(p^*)/K.
$$
The <sigmoidal delayed ventilation threshold> follows from the <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation>, $\lambda+A+Be^{-\lambda\tau}=0$. At an imaginary root $\lambda=i\omega$, separate real and imaginary parts:
$$
A+B\cos(\omega\tau)=0,\qquad\omega-B\sin(\omega\tau)=0.
$$
For $B>A>0$, the first positive crossing has
$$
\boxed{\omega_c=\sqrt{B^2-A^2},\qquad\tau_c=\frac{\arccos(-A/B)}{\sqrt{B^2-A^2}}.}
$$
At zero delay the scalar equation is stable. No root can cross the imaginary axis before this first crossing, and zero itself is not a root. To check the crossing direction, implicit differentiation gives
$$
\lambda'=-\frac{\lambda(\lambda+A)}{1+\tau(\lambda+A)},\qquad
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}\lambda'(\tau_c)=\frac{\omega_c^2}{(1+A\tau_c)^2+\omega_c^2\tau_c^2}>0.}
$$
All subsequent imaginary crossings have the same direction. Thus the equilibrium is linearly stable below $\tau_c$ and unstable above it. This establishes the oscillatory linear threshold; the full nonlinear <Hopf bifurcation> additionally requires the usual nondegeneracy conditions. If $B\leq A$, no finite imaginary-root crossing occurs and the equilibrium is stable for every finite delay. For the stated response, $B/A=n\theta^n/(\theta^n+(p^*)^n)$, showing explicitly when the gain can exceed instantaneous damping.

If $A\ll B$, then $\omega_c\approx B$ and $\omega_c\tau_c\approx\pi/2$. Consequently the small-amplitude period near onset is $\boxed{2\pi/\omega_c\approx4\tau_c}$. The comparison is to the critical delay near the first instability, rather than an arbitrary delay far beyond onset. A longer circulatory transit time can move the physiological system across this delayed-feedback threshold, providing a mechanism for periodic breathing associated with impaired cardiac output.
